WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.601
The merchant dashboard reads from two followers, r1 and r2.

2
00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:13.167
They're asynchronous: r1 is 300 milliseconds behind, and r2 is 900.

3
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.423
A read on a follower shows the data as of its position in the leader's log.

4
00:00:18.423 --> 00:00:20.432
Right now, all three are at 0.

5
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.642
At one second, the merchant asks for a refund of payment 41.

6
00:00:24.642 --> 00:00:27.047
The leader commits it at position 2.

7
00:00:27.267 --> 00:00:30.105
Ten milliseconds later the page reloads.

8
00:00:30.105 --> 00:00:33.227
The router sends the read to r1, still at 0.

9
00:00:33.227 --> 00:00:34.647
No refund is listed.

10
00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:36.729
The merchant clicks Refund again.

11
00:00:36.729 --> 00:00:41.301
What they needed is read your writes: your own reads always show your own writes.

12
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:43.650
The fix is to carry the position.

13
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.947
The leader returns position 2, and the session keeps it as a token.

14
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:52.388
Before a read, the router checks the follower against the token.

15
00:00:52.388 --> 00:00:55.817
r1 is at 0, below 2, so the read goes to the leader.

16
00:00:56.067 --> 00:01:01.044
Once r1 reaches position 2, the merchant's reads go back to r1.

17
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.081
Now an operator watches the same payment.

18
00:01:04.081 --> 00:01:07.201
The operator wrote nothing, so its token is 0.

19
00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.347
The router takes turns.

20
00:01:09.347 --> 00:01:11.011
r1 shows the refund.

21
00:01:11.011 --> 00:01:13.674
Then r2, still at 0, shows none.

22
00:01:13.674 --> 00:01:15.588
Then r1 shows it again.

23
00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:18.640
The refund appears, vanishes and comes back.

24
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:24.508
The missing guarantee is monotonic reads: you never see older data than you've already seen.

25
00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:30.248
Same fix, one more rule: the session also keeps the highest position it has read.

26
00:01:30.248 --> 00:01:34.197
That makes the operator's token 2, so the router skips r2.

27
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.217
Both fixes are the same move.

28
00:01:36.217 --> 00:01:39.725
The reader carries a position, and never reads below it.
